    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}\n\nWhen the ceramic has a much lower coefficient of thermal expansion than the metal, cooling induces tensile stresses in the ceramic, which is weak in tension, often causing the ceramic itself to crack (cohesive failure). Adhesive failure at the bond or cohesive failure of the metal is less likely because the metal is more ductile and the stress concentration primarily affects the brittle ceramic.",
