      "generated_answer": "The first inversion of a major triad has the third of the chord in the bass. In the key of C major, the first inversion of a C major triad would be E-G-C, with E in the bass. Among the options, only [GB_e]4 (option A) represents a G major triad in first inversion (B in the bass would be ^B, but here it's _e, indicating a lower E, suggesting a C major triad with E in the bass—though notation is tricky). However, interpreting the accidentals and pitches carefully, [GB_e] suggests G, B, E—which is a G major triad with E in the bass, i.e., first inversion of G major. Since the key is C, G major is the dominant, and this fits. Thus, A is correct.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
