      "generated_answer": "We are given an undirected graph G with N vertices and M edges. The graph is \"good\" initially, meaning that for K given pairs (x_i, y_i), there is no path between x_i and y_i in G.\n\nWe are then given Q queries. In each query, we are to add an edge (p_i, q_i) to G and check if the resulting graph is still \"good\" — i.e., for all K pairs (x_j, y_j), there is still no path between x_j and y_j.\n\n### Key Insight:\n- The graph G is initially good: for each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), x_i and y_i are in different connected components.\n- When we add an edge (p, q), we are merging the two connected components that contain p and q.\n- After adding this edge, the graph becomes **not good** if and only if **at least one** of the forbidden pairs (x_i, y_i) now lies in the same connected component (i.e., there is a path between them).\n\nSo, the problem reduces to:\n\n1. Find all connected components of the original graph G (ignoring self-loops and duplicates? But note: multi-edges and self-loops are allowed, but self-loops don't connect different vertices, so we can ignore self-loops for connectivity).\n2. For each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), we note that they are in different components (given).\n3. For each query (p_i, q_i):\n   - Find the connected components of p_i and q_i.\n   - If adding an edge between them merges two components, then check: **does this merge cause any forbidden pair (x_j, y_j) to become connected?**\n   - But note: we cannot iterate over all K pairs for each query (K, Q up to 200,000) — that would be O(K*Q) = 40e9, too slow.\n\n### Better approach:\n\nWe can use the idea of **union-find with \"component tracking\"**.\n\nBut note: we are not actually going to modify the graph permanently. We are just answering Q independent queries: \"If we add edge (p_i, q_i) to the original graph G, is the result still good?\"\n\nSo we can precompute the connected components of G (using DSU or DFS/BFS).\n\nLet’s do:\n\n- Use DSU to compute connected components of G. But note: we are not going to union for the queries — we are just simulating what would happen if we added one edge.\n\nSo:\n\n1. Precompute connected components of G (using DSU or BFS/DFS). Let `comp[i]` be the component id of vertex i.\n2. For each forbidden pair (x_j, y_j), we know that `comp[x_j] != comp[y_j]`.\n3. Now, when we add an edge between p and q, we are merging the two components `comp[p]` and `comp[q]`.\n   - Then, any forbidden pair (x, y) that has one endpoint in `comp[p]` and the other in `comp[q]` will now be connected.\n   - Also, if both x and y are in `comp[p]` (or both in `comp[q]`), then they were already connected? But wait — the original graph is good, so no forbidden pair is connected initially. So if both x and y are in `comp[p]`, then they were already in the same component? That would violate the condition. So that cannot happen.\n\nWait! Important: the original graph G is good → for every forbidden pair (x_j, y_j), they are in different components.\n\nTherefore, a forbidden pair (x_j, y_j) can only be \"broken\" (i.e., become connected) by the new edge if and only if:\n- x_j is in the component of p, and y_j is in the component of q, OR\n- x_j is in the component of q, and y_j is in the component of p.\n\nIn other words: the edge (p, q) connects two components C1 and C2. Then, if there exists any forbidden pair (x, y) such that x is in C1 and y is in C2, then adding the edge (p, q) will connect x and y → so the graph is not good.\n\nBut note: what if the forbidden pair (x, y) has x in C1 and y in some other component C3 (not C2)? Then adding the edge between C1 and C2 doesn't connect x and y.\n\nSo the condition for a query (p, q) to result in a \"No\" is:\n- The two components of p and q (call them C1 and C2) are different (if they are same, adding an edge within a component doesn't change connectivity) → then we merge C1 and C2.\n- And there exists at least one forbidden pair (x, y) such that x ∈ C1 and y ∈ C2.\n\nSo we can precompute:\n\n- `comp_id[i]` for each vertex i: the connected component id (we can use DSU with union-find and assign a representative for each component).\n- For each component, we can store the set of forbidden pairs that have one endpoint in this component? Actually, we don't need the set, we need to know: for each pair of components (C1, C2), is there a forbidden pair (x, y) with x in C1 and y in C2?\n\nBut we have up to 200,000 components? Actually, at most N=200,000 vertices, but connected components are at most 200,000, but likely fewer.\n\nBut we have K up to 200,000 forbidden pairs.\n\nWe can do:\n\n- Group forbidden pairs by the two components they connect. For each forbidden pair (x, y), let c1 = comp_id[x], c2 = comp_id[y]. Without loss of generality, let c1 < c2 (so we can store as a tuple (min(c1,c2), max(c1,c2))).\n\nThen, create a set (or a dictionary) of these \"inter-component forbidden edges\": `forbidden_pairs_by_components = set()`\n\nBut note: multiple forbidden pairs might connect the same two components? The constraint says: \"i ≠ j ⟹ {x_i, y_i} ≠ {x_j, y_j}\" — so the unordered pairs of vertices are distinct, but two different forbidden pairs might connect the same two components? Yes, for example: (1,5) and (2,5) might both be forbidden, and if 1 and 2 are in the same component, then both connect component of 1 (which is same as 2) to component of 5. So we can have multiple forbidden pairs between the same two components.\n\nBut we only care: is there at least one? So we can store a set of component pairs (c1, c2) (with c1 < c2) that have at least one forbidden pair.\n\nSo:\n\nPrecomputation:\n1. Build graph from M edges (ignore self-loops? Actually, self-loops don't affect connectivity, so we can ignore them for connectivity. But we must include multi-edges? They don't change connectivity either. So we can use DSU to merge connected vertices, and ignore duplicates and self-loops).\n2. Use DSU to compute connected components. We'll assign each vertex a root (representative).\n3. For each forbidden pair (x, y):\n   - get comp_x = find(x), comp_y = find(y)\n   - if comp_x == comp_y: this should not happen (given that G is good), so we can skip or error? But constraint says \"there is no path\", so they are in different components. So we assume comp_x != comp_y.\n   - store a tuple (min(comp_x, comp_y), max(comp_x, comp_y)) in a set called `forbidden_component_pairs`\n4. For each query (p, q):\n   - comp_p = find(p), comp_q = find(q)\n   - if comp_p == comp_q: then adding the edge doesn't change connectivity → so no new connection between components → then no forbidden pair becomes connected → answer \"Yes\"\n   - else:\n        let c1 = min(comp_p, comp_q), c2 = max(comp_p, comp_q)\n        if (c1, c2) in forbidden_component_pairs:\n            then adding this edge connects two components that had at least one forbidden pair → so now there is a path between some forbidden pair → answer \"No\"\n        else:\n            answer \"Yes\"\n\nWait: is that sufficient?\n\nConsider: we are merging two components C1 and C2. Then, any forbidden pair that has one vertex in C1 and one in C2 becomes connected. We have stored the component pairs (c1, c2) that have at least one forbidden pair. So if (c1, c2) is in the set, then there is at least one forbidden pair that will become connected → so the graph is not good → \"No\".\n\nBut what if there are multiple forbidden pairs between C1 and C2? We only store one tuple (c1, c2). That's fine: we only care if there is at least one.\n\nBut note: what if the new edge connects C1 and C2, and there is a forbidden pair (x, y) where x is in C1 and y is in C3 (a third component)? Then adding C1-C2 doesn't connect x and y. So we don't care.\n\nTherefore, the condition is: the only forbidden pairs that become connected are those that have one endpoint in C1 and the other in C2.\n\nSo the above logic is correct.\n\n### Implementation:\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read N