th path compression and union by rank.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nNote: DSU for 1-indexed vertices.\n\nWe'll implement a simple DSU class or just functions.\n\nWe'll use list for parent and rank.\n\nWe'll do:\n\nparent[i] = i for i in range(1, N+1)\n\nWe'll create an array of size N+1 (index 0 unused).\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nWe'll use union by rank and path compression.\n\nBut note: we only need `find` for each vertex at the end. We don't need to do union during queries, only during building the graph.\n\nSo:\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read N, M\n2. Initialize DSU: parent[1..N] = i, rank[1..N] = 0\n3. For each of M edges:\n      read u, v\n      if u != v: union(u, v)\n4. Read K\n5. forbidden_pairs_set = set()\n   for each of K lines:\n        read x, y\n        rx = find(x), ry = find(y)\n        if rx != ry:\n            c1, c2 = min(rx, ry), max(rx, ry)\n            forbidden_pairs_set.add( (c1, c2) )\n6. Read Q\n7. For each of Q lines:\n        read p, q\n        rp = find(p), rq = find(q)\n        if rp == rq:\n            print(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            c1, c2 = min(rp, rq), max(rp, rq)\n            if (c1, c2) in forbidden_pairs_set:\n                print(\"No\")\n            else:\n                print(\"Yes\")\n\nWe are done.\n\nLet's write the code accordingly.\n\nNote: We must do path compression in find for efficiency.\n\nWe'll implement DSU with path compression and union by rank (though union by rank is not strictly necessary for correctness, but helps performance).\n\nBut note: M can be up to 200,000, and we do M unions and then 200,000 finds (for forbidden pairs) and 200,000 finds for queries → total about 600,000 find operations → with path compression, it's efficient.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nWe'll write the DSU functions inside the main code.\n\nWe'll use 1-indexed.\n\nWe'll create arrays: parent = [0] * (N+1) and rank = [0] * (N+1)\n\nInitialize: for i in range(1, N+1): parent[i] = i\n\nThen:\n\ndef find(x):\n    if parent[x] != x:\n        parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n    return parent[x]\n\ndef union(x, y):\n    rx = find(x)\n    ry = find(y)\n    if rx == ry:\n        return\n    if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n        rx, ry = ry, rx\n    parent[ry] = rx\n    if rank[rx] == rank[ry]:\n        rank[rx] += 1\n\nBut note: we don't care about the structure of the tree, only the representative. So union by rank is for efficiency.\n\nWe'll do that.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nWe'll read from stdin.\n\nWe'll write the entire program.\n\nNote: we must not output anything extra.\n\nLet's code.\n\n\n\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine whether adding a specific edge to an undirected graph maintains its \"good\" property. A graph is considered \"good\" if there is no path between any of the given forbidden vertex pairs. The solution involves efficiently checking connectivity in the graph after each potential edge addition.\n\n### Approach\n1. **Problem Analysis**: The problem involves an undirected graph with N vertices and M edges. The graph is initially \"good,\" meaning no path exists between any of the K specified forbidden vertex pairs. For each of Q queries, we need to check if adding a new edge between two vertices (p_i, q_i) would make the graph no longer \"good\" (i.e., if it creates a path between any forbidden pair).\n\n2. **Key Insight**: The graph remains \"good\" after adding an edge (p_i, q_i) if and only if the addition does not connect any two vertices that form a forbidden pair. This translates to checking whether the new edge merges two connected components that contain at least one forbidden pair.\n\n3. **Algorithm Selection**: \n   - **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union - DSU)**: This data structure efficiently manages and queries connected components in the graph. We use it to:\n     - Initially compute connected components of the graph.\n     - For each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), record the pair of component representatives (min, max) to avoid redundancy.\n   - **Query Processing**: For each query (p_i, q_i), find the components of p_i and q_i. If they are the same, adding the edge doesn't change connectivity (answer \"Yes\"). If they differ, check if the merged components (min, max) of p_i and q_i contain any forbidden pair. If yes, answer \"No\"; otherwise, \"Yes\".\n\n4. **Complexity Analysis**: \n   - **Initialization**: Building the DSU for M edges takes O(M α(N)) time, where α is the inverse Ackermann function (nearly constant).\n   - **Processing Forbidden Pairs**: O(K α(N)) for finding components of K pairs.\n   - **Query Processing**: O(Q α(N)) for Q queries.\n   - Overall complexity is O((M + K + Q) α(N)), which is efficient for the given constraints.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it))\n    M = int(next(it))\n    \n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    rank = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return\n",
