    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countSubMultisets(nums, l, r):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Count frequency of each number\n    from collections import Counter\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    \n    # DP array: dp[i] = number of ways to achieve sum i\n    dp = [0] * (r + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # empty multiset\n    \n    # Process each unique number\n    for num, count in freq.items():\n        if num == 0:\n            # Special case: zeros can be included any number of times (0 to count)\n            # Each zero doesn't change the sum, but multiplies the number of ways\n            dp[0] = (dp[0] * (count + 1)) % MOD\n            continue\n            \n        # We'll use a temporary array to avoid using updated values in same iteration\n        # We do a backward iteration for each number to avoid overcounting\n        # This is similar to bounded knapsack with frequency constraint\n        new_dp = dp[:]  # copy current state\n        \n        # For each possible current sum\n        for s in range(r + 1):\n            if dp[s] == 0:\n                continue\n            # Try adding 1, 2, ..., count copies of num\n            for k in range(1, count + 1):\n                new_sum = s + k * num\n                if new_sum > r:\n                    break\n                new_dp[new_sum] = (new_dp[new_sum] + dp[s]) % MOD\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n    \n    # Sum up counts for all sums in [l, r]\n    result = 0\n    for i in range(l, r + 1):\n        result = (result + dp[i]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
