 if it is entirely covered by any of the original rectangles. If yes, add its area to the total.\n4. **Complexity Analysis**: The number of unique x and y coordinates is at most 2N (200 in the worst case). The grid has O(N²) cells, and checking each cell against N rectangles results in O(N³) time complexity, which is feasible given N ≤ 100.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    rectangles = []\n    x_coords = set()\n    y_coords = set()\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        line = data[i].split()\n        a, b, c, d = map(int, line)\n        rectangles.append((a, b, c, d))\n        x_coords.add(a)\n        x_coords.add(b)\n        y_coords.add(c)\n        y_coords.add(d)\n    \n    x_coords = sorted(x_coords)\n    y_coords = sorted(y_coords)\n    \n    total_area = 0\n    \n    for i in range(len(x_coords) - 1):\n        x1 = x_coords[i]\n        x2 = x_coords[i + 1]\n        for j in range(len(y_coords) - 1):\n            y1 = y_coords[j]\n            y2 = y_coords[j + 1]\n            \n            covered = False\n            for a, b, c, d in rectangles:\n                if a <= x1 and x2 <= b and c <= y1 and y2 <= d:\n                    covered = True\n                    break\n            \n            if covered:\n                total_area += (x2 - x1) * (y2 - y1)\n    \n    print(total_area)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Reading Input**: The input is read from standard input, parsing the number of rectangles and their coordinates.\n2. **Coordinate Collection**: Unique x and y coordinates from all rectangle boundaries are collected into sets to avoid duplicates.\n3. **Sorting Coordinates**: The unique coordinates are sorted to form intervals that partition the plane into non-overlapping rectangular cells.\n4. **Area Calculation**: For each cell (defined by consecutive x and y coordinates), we check if it is entirely contained within any of the original rectangles. If it is, the cell's area (width × height) is added to the total area.\n5. **Output**: The accumulated total area, representing the union of all rectangles, is printed.\n\nThis approach efficiently computes the union area by leveraging coordinate compression to handle overlaps without complex geometric calculations, ensuring correctness and optimal performance within the given constraints.",
