ount) for subsequences of length j ending at i.\n    # But min_diff can be many values. However, n <= 50, k <= 50, and min_diff is determined by differences between elements.\n    # There are at most O(n^2) distinct min_diff values per state, so total states O(n^2 * k * n^2) = O(50^4) = 6.25e6 worst-case, acceptable in Pyton with optimization.\n    \n    # Instead, use: dp[i][j] = dictionary mapping min_diff to count.\n    # Transition: for each i, for each j from 1 to k, for each prev < i:\n    #   new_min = min(prev_min, nums[i] - nums[prev])\n    #   But we need prev_min from dp[prev][j-1]\n    \n    # Initialize: dp[i][1] = {float('inf'): 1}  # min_diff for single element is infinity (or we handle separately)\n    # Actually for j=1, no pairs, so min_diff is not defined. But when we add second element, min_diff = diff.\n    \n    # Let's do: dp[i][j] = dict of min_diff values for subsequences of length j ending at i.\n    # For j=1: we can store a dummy value, but better start from j=2.\n    \n    # Revised: \n    # dp[i][j] = dict: key = minimum difference in the subsequence, value = count\n    # For j=2: dp[i][2][nums[i]-nums[prev]] += 1 for each prev < i\n    # For j>2: for each prev < i, for each (min_diff, cnt) in dp[prev][j-1]:\n    #   new_min = min(min_diff, nums[i]-nums[prev])\n    #   dp[i][j][new_min] += cnt\n    \n    # Then sum over all i, all (min_diff, cnt) in dp[i][k]: min_diff * cnt\n    \n    # But memory and time: O(n^2 * k * n) = O(n^3 * k) = 50^4 = 6.25e6,",
