    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "For a convex polyhedron with only regular pentagonal and hexagonal faces, Euler’s formula (V − E + F = 2) and face-edge relationships apply. Let F = k + m, and count edges: each pentagon contributes 5 edges, each hexagon 6, but each edge is shared by two faces, so 5k + 6m = 2E. Also, each vertex in such a polyhedron (e.g., a fullerene) has degree 3, so 3V = 5k + 6m. Substituting into Euler’s formula gives k = 12. This is a well-known result: any convex polyhedron composed solely of regular pentagons and hexagons (like a soccer ball or fullerene) must have exactly 12 pentagonal faces.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
