    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_29",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nE ⊃ F\nG ⊃ ~F / ~G ∨ ~E\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when E, F, and G are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when E and G are true and F is false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when F and G are true and E is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To test validity using an indirect truth table, assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false, then check for contradiction.  \nPremises: E ⊃ F (1), G ⊃ ~F (2); Conclusion: ~G ∨ ~E is false ⇒ both ~G and ~E are false ⇒ G and E are true.  \nIf G and E are true, then from (1): E ⊃ F true ⇒ F must be true; from (2): G ⊃ ~F true ⇒ ~F must be true ⇒ F false — contradiction.  \nWait—recheck: If conclusion ~G ∨ ~E is false, then G = T and E = T. Then (1) E⊃F requires F = T; (2) G⊃~F requires ~F = T ⇒ F = F — contradiction. So no assignment makes premises true and conclusion false ⇒ argument is valid.  \nBut check option B: E, F, G all true: premises: T⊃T = T, T⊃~T = T⊃F = F ⇒ second premise false, so not a counterexample.  \nOption C: E=T, G=T, F=F: (1) T⊃F = F ⇒ first premise false.  \nOption D: F=T, G=T, E=F: (1) F⊃T = T, (2) T⊃~T = F ⇒ second premise false.  \nOnly when both premises are true: need E⊃F = T and G⊃~F = T. Cases:  \n- If F=T, then E can be T or F, and G must be F (since G⊃F is false unless G=F). Then conclusion ~G ∨ ~E = T ∨ ? = T.  \n- If F=F, then E must be F (to make E⊃F true), and G can be T or F. Then ~G ∨ ~E = ? ∨ T = T.  \nSo conclusion always true when premises true ⇒ valid.  \nThus answer is A.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 200,
        "output_tokens": 427,
