      "generated_answer": "To compute the product $P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1)$, we recognize that $\\omega^7 = 1$. Since $\\omega^7 - 1 = (\\omega - 1)(\\omega^6 + \\omega^5 + \\dots + 1) = 0$ and $\\omega \\neq 1$, the values $\\omega^k$ for $k=0, \\dots, 6$ are the roots of $x^7 - 1 = 0$. Let $f(x) = x^3 + x + 1$; then $P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 f(\\omega^k)$. Using the property that $\\prod_{k=0}^6 (x - \\omega^k) = x^7 - 1$, we note that $f(x) = (x - \\alpha)(x - \\beta)(x - \\gamma)$ where $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ are the roots of $x^3+x+1=0$. Thus $P = \\prod_{\\alpha} \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^k - \\alpha) = \\prod_{\\alpha} (-1)^7 ( \\alpha^7 - 1) = -(\\alpha^7 - 1)(\\beta^7 - 1)(\\gamma^7 - 1)$. With $\\alpha^3 = -\\alpha - 1$, we find $\\alpha^7 = \\alpha(\\alpha^3)^2 = \\alpha(\\alpha^2 + 2\\alpha + 1) = \\alpha^3 + 2\\alpha^2 + \\alpha = (-\\alpha - 1) + 2\\alpha^2 + \\alpha = 2\\alpha^2 - 1$, so $\\alpha^7 - 1 = 2\\alpha^2 - 2$. The product is $-(2\\alpha^2 - 2)(2\\beta^2 - 2)(2\\gamma^2 - 2) = -8(\\alpha^2-1)(\\beta^2-1)(\\gamma^2-1) = -8 g(1)g(-1)$, where $g(x) = x^3+x+1$. Evaluating $g(1)=3$ and $g(-1)=-1$, we get $-8(3)(-1) = 24$.\n\n\\boxed{24}",
