      "generated_answer": "We analyze when the greedy algorithm (using as many quarters as possible, then dimes, then pennies) yields the minimal number of coins for amount $ N $ cents. Let $ q = \\lfloor N/25 \\rfloor $, $ r = N \\bmod 25 $, then greedy uses $ q + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\bmod 10) $ coins. An alternative using $ q-1 $ quarters (if $ q \\ge 1 $) can use up to two more dimes and some pennies: the best such alternative uses $ \\lceil r/10 \\rceil $ dimes and $ 10\\lceil r/10 \\rceil - r $ pennies, totaling $ (q-1) + \\lceil r/10 \\rceil + (10\\lceil r/10 \\rceil - r) = q - 1 + 2\\lceil r/10 \\rceil - r $ coins. Greedy is optimal iff $ \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\bmod 10) \\le 2\\lceil r/10 \\rceil - r $, which simplifies to checking $ r \\in \\{0,1,\\dots,9,20,21,\\dots,24\\} $. So for each block of 25 cents, 20 values of $ r $ are good. From 1 to 1000, there are $ \\lfloor 1000/25 \\rfloor = 40 $ full blocks, each contributing 20 good $ N $, and the remainder $ N = 1000 $ is $ r = 0 $, already included. Total good $ N $: $ 40 \\times 20 = 800 $. Note $ N = 0 $ is excluded, but $ r = 0 $ corresponds to $ N = 25,50,\\dots,1000 $, all ≥25 and valid.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{800}\n$$",
